          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.613,
            0.859,
            0.675
          ],
          "content": "| x | 1 | 2 | 5 | 7 | 9 | 12 | 20 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| y | 3 |  |  | 15 |  |  |  |",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.133,
                0.591,
                0.793,
                0.611
              ],
              "content": "Identify other points on the line by completing the following table."
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_table_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.698,
            0.886,
            0.796
          ],
          "content": "Note that (1, 3) and (7, 15) are also the points on the line y = 2x + 1. Plot the points on the line. You may use a graph paper for this task. Observe that if a point lies on a line, its coordinates must satisfy the equation of the line. Thus, we can verify that (7, 15) lies on the line y = 2x + 1 by substituting x = 7 and y = 15 in the equation."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.796,
            0.888,
            0.895
          ],
          "content": "**Example 12:** Let us plot the points (–1, –3), (0, 0), (1, 3), (3, 9), (4, 12) in the coordinate plane on a graph paper as shown in Fig. 2.6. Join the points (–1, –3) and (4, 12) using a ruler. Doing so, observe that all five points lie on a straight line. Can you guess the equation of this line by looking at the relationship between the x and y coordinates of each point?"
        }
      ]
    }
